10. [PAPER-NOTE — 2026-09-21] RUNTIME-ORACLE SCOPE — report in the paper
   (threats-to-validity / design-scope / limitations). ToFu is a RUNTIME (dynamic)
   analyzer; its oracle observes runtime behavior of a tool driven by adversarial
   input. Three vuln classes are DELIBERATELY out of scope because they have NO
   runtime event a dynamic oracle can observe — a principled boundary, NOT a
   weakness; CodeQL/static tools are the correct complement:
     1. CORS-permissive (CWE-942): static server CONFIG; no tool input triggers it.
     2. Regexp / sanitization CORRECTNESS (incomplete-hostname-regexp, missing-anchor,
        incomplete-multi-char-sanitization): latent code weakness; generic fuzz inputs
        don't manifest it and the oracle can't know the intended contract.
     3. TOCTOU file-system-race (CWE-367): needs CONCURRENT access to open the
        check→use window; ToFu fuzzes one input at a time (execution-model limit).
   ADDED 2026-09-21 (these DO have a runtime signal): check_introspection_exfil
   (CWE-201, sensitive-read↔outbound-send correlation) + check_reflected_output
   (CWE-79/116, dangerous input echoed unescaped). prototype-pollution/ReDoS/XXE
   already HAVE oracles → those misses are reachability, not oracle-absence. Frame
   ToFu's ~35–40% blind spot as this principled runtime/static division of labor.

   SINK + SANITIZER CONSTRUCTION — DEFEND in the paper (2026-09-21). Verified counts
   (pin the exact reported number by DEFINING "sink" first): 21 sink TRACER classes
   (hooked dangerous operations: FileAccess, FileWrite, NetworkExecution, Command/
   Code/SQLExecution, Deserialization, XXE, ReDoS, WeakHash/PRNG, NonceReuse,
   TempFileLeak, SensitiveDataLogging, LogInjection, SSLCertValidation,
   UnrestrictedIPBinding, UnverifiedCodeDownload, BlockingEventLoopCall,
   LoopCondition, DataAmplification) == the 21 sink FAMILIES in the corpus scan;
   ~30 distinct res.* evidence channels; **73 registered AVAILABLE_SANITIZERS**
   (75 check_ defined; 2 unregistered = check_invalid_git_ref/check_unsafe_s;
   10 BASE_MANDATORY always-on + 63 LLM/evidence-selected). The 2 newest
   (exfil, reflected_output) added sanitizers only, NO new sink.
   DEFENSE the reviewers will demand ("why THESE sinks/sanitizers, not others?"):
   ground the taxonomy, don't present it as ad-hoc — (a) each maps to a CWE; (b)
   derived from the MCP tool threat surface + observed sink families in the scan
   (the 21 are what actually occur in the corpus, not invented); (c) prior-work
   coverage (Viper's 3 classes ⊂ ours; CodeQL rule families cross-checked); (d) the
   2 additions were miss-analysis-DRIVEN (evidence, not guesswork). State the
   mandatory-vs-selected split (attribution trap, CLAUDE.md §2). Open q to answer:
   completeness ("is 21 the right basis?") + why some CWEs have no oracle (item 10
   scope note above).

11. [PAPER-NOTE — 2026-09-21] AUTO-TRIAGE CLASSIFIER — report in the paper
   (methodology / results). Reproducible implementation: eval/triage_findings.py.
   It runs over the RUN INDEX (runs.<arm>.jsonl), not prose. Pipeline:
     - INPUT: every config with finding_hits>0 = the silent-bug oracle already
       fired (adversarial input tripped a sanitizer AND the tool returned success).
     - CLASS MAP (signal vs noise): each triggered sanitizer → a category.
       KEPT (security-exploitable): path_traversal→CWE-22; command_injection /
       blocked_command_bypass→CWE-78; SSRF family (introspection_ssrf,
       url_ssrf_ip_ranges, url_hostname_canonicalization,
       url_alternative_ip_representations, strict_url_security_contract)→CWE-918;
       log_injection→CWE-117; hardcoded_credentials→CWE-798; temp_file_leak→CWE-377.
       DROPPED (robustness/noise): type_confusion, unexpected_crash, invalid_json,
       resource_exhaustion, incomplete_cleanup, fd_leaks, weak_prng — quality/DoS
       or mandatory-layer artifacts (CLAUDE.md §2: 10 always-on sanitizers fire
       regardless of LLM selection → not evidence of a targeted vuln).
     - SEVERITY PICK: label a config by its highest-severity kept class
       (path-traversal + crash → path-traversal).
     - DEDUP: count distinct (repo, tool, class) — same tool across runs = 1 vuln.
     - CONFIDENCE TIER by exec_success_rate (fraction of execs the tool swallowed
       the adversarial input): ≥0.5 = high-confidence (consistent, not flaky);
       any >0 = full candidate set.
   RESULT (set_e2): 600 candidates / 281 high-confidence / 148 repos. State it as
   a noise filter + ranking, NOT a confirmer — candidates still need hand-
   verification + a false-positive rate; mandatory layer must be subtracted before
   any LLM-selection (RQ2/RQ3) claim.

12. [RESOLVED — 2026-09-23] NODE TARGET SERVERS LEAKED ON EVERY JS CONFIG.
   SYMPTOM: VM had 663 orphaned `node src/runners/coverage_wrapper.js
   eval/targets/<repo>/.../index.js`, all ppid=1, 44-48h old (set_e2 / rerun),
   ~47 GB RSS, 5 GB free of 38. Killed by hand. Same class as item 1's "92
   orphans" follow-up (a). The laptop still has ~180 of them from local runs.
   REPRODUCED (VM, 3 node configs, --time 20, arm leakcheck-baseline, ORIGINAL
   code): normal exit 3/3 servers leaked; forced timeout 1/3; Ctrl-C 2/3.
   ROOT CAUSE, two independent holes:
     (a) JSRunner Popen(start_new_session=True) puts node in its OWN session
         (needed so its startup watchdog can killpg the server tree), so
         run_eval's os.killpg(<fuzzer pgid>) on timeout/Ctrl-C never reached it.
     (b) libFuzzer ends at -max_total_time with a C-level exit(): fuzzer.
         _cleanup_runner() -> JSRunner.stop() never runs, nor do Python atexit
         handlers. The wrapper never noticed: it writes stdout once
         (SOCKET_READY), never reads its real stdin, and the listening socket
         keeps its event loop alive forever. (Servers that later write to the
         dead stdout pipe die of EPIPE -- why it was 663 and not every config.)
     Not libFuzzer forks (no -fork/-jobs used).
   FIX (layered; any one suffices for the common case):
     1. coverage_wrapper.js ORPHAN GUARD: on real-stdin EOF (kernel closes it the
        instant the fuzzer dies, any cause), ppid change, or SIGTERM -> unlink
        its socket + SIGKILL its whole process group (node + target children).
     2. JSRunner: Linux `setpriv --pdeathsig TERM` prefix (procreap.
        pdeathsig_prefix; main-thread only, since PDEATHSIG follows the forking
        THREAD). NOT KILL -- measured: KILL killed node before the guard ran and
        orphaned the target's own child. stop() now always SIGKILLs the group
        after SIGTERM, then sweeps by the unique FUZZER_SOCKET_PATH env; start()
        reaps a crashed predecessor before restarting; atexit stops live runners.
     3. run_eval.one(): unique TOFU_REAP_TAG=<config>.<uuid> in the fuzzer env,
        inherited by every descendant (not stripped by scrub_secrets); after each
        config (normal / timeout / harness exception) and in _abort, SIGKILL
        every process whose /proc/PID/environ carries it. Catches setsid'd
        grandchildren too. Index field `reaped` = how many the sweep had to kill
        (nonzero => layers 1-2 missed something). SIGHUP now -> _abort too
        (run_eval + run_repo24), so a closed tmux/ssh reaps instead of orphaning.
     Code: src/runners/procreap.py (new), js_runner.py, coverage_wrapper.js,
     eval/run_eval.py, eval/run_repo24.py; tests/test_procreap.py (new).
   VERIFIED on the VM (py3.13, node v22.23.2), leftover `node coverage_wrapper`
   count after each run, arms eval/state/runs.leakcheck-*.jsonl:
     normal exit           0  (reaped=0 x3: guard/PDEATHSIG got them first)
     forced timeout        0  (TOFU_KILL_GRACE=-95 -> killed at 25s mid-fuzz)
     Ctrl-C (SIGINT group) 0  (and 0 fuzzers)
     sweep ONLY (TOFU_NO_ORPHAN_GUARD=1 TOFU_NO_PDEATHSIG=1): normal 0 (reaped=1
       x3), timeout 0 (reaped=1 x3), Ctrl-C 0  -> layer 3 alone is sufficient.
     No new /tmp/mcp_fuzz_*.sock left behind (1641 stale ones predate the fix).
   tests/test_procreap.py::test_js_server_dies... FAILS with both layers off
   (2 survivors: node + its child) and passes with the fix, VM and laptop.
   macOS: `ps -E` no longer exposes other processes' env, so layer 3 is
   Linux-only; the laptop relies on layer 1 (+atexit).
   RESIDUAL GAP: a target wedged in a synchronous JS loop runs no handler, so
   layers 1-2 cannot fire; only layer 3 (run_eval, Linux) catches it. A target
   that CLEARS its env and setsid()s escapes layer 3 too.
   SIDE NOTES: (i) tests now 160 passed / 1 skipped on the laptop (CLAUDE.md's
   "66 passed, 1 skipped" is stale); VM also fails test_no_undefined_names_
   anywhere = pyflakes RecursionError on eval/targets/**/.venv, pre-existing
   item 1(c). (ii) test_introspection_sanitizer.py:375 leaves
   os.environ["PATH"]="/tmp/malicious_bin" for every later test.
   (iii) run_eval.parse_log ignores LLM_SELECTION_CACHE_HIT -> cached runs are
   indexed llm_selection=unknown (seen on all leakcheck runs); deep24/repo24
   reuse the cache, so their indexes will misreport selection health until fixed.

13. [RESOLVED — 2026-09-23] CACHED RUNS INDEXED AS llm_selection=unknown; AND
   THE REPLAY CACHE HOLDS FALLBACK SEEDS.
   SYMPTOM: every run served by the LLM replay cache (src/llm.py,
   TOFU_LLM_CACHE_DIR, default eval/state/llm_cache) was indexed
   llm_selection=unknown / llm_seeds=unknown with no n_selected/n_seeds, because
   run_eval.parse_log knew only LLM_SELECTION_OK/FAILED and LLM_SEEDGEN_OK/
   FALLBACK, while a cached run logs LLM_SELECTION_CACHE_HIT / LLM_SEEDGEN_CACHE_HIT.
   Seen on all item-12 leakcheck runs. deep24/repo24 reuse the cache -> their
   whole index would have misreported LLM-stage health.
   SECOND FINDING (why a seed hit is NOT reported as "ok"): eval/gen_llm_cache.py
   retro-built the cache from corpus seed_*.json, and fuzzer._setup_corpus_seeds
   writes seed_*.json for WHATEVER seeds it got -- including the deterministic
   fallback. Cross-checked against the gpt41-corpus logs (VM, 5022 entries,
   4122 with seeds): llm=3670, fallback=12, unknown=440 (source log has neither
   marker, mostly runs that were themselves cache hits or timed out). 3 of the
   12 fallback entries are deep24 configs:
     CodeLogicIncEngineering__codelogic-mcp-server__lineai-graph-validate-change-scope
     IncomeStreamSurfer__roo-code-memory-bank-mcp-server__append_memory_bank_entry
     IncomeStreamSurfer__roo-code-memory-bank-mcp-server__read_memory_bank_file
   The SELECTION cache is clean: it is only written on the LLM_SELECTION_OK path
   (live _cache_update; gen_llm_cache's "Sanitizers selected:" is OK-only).
   FIX:
     - parse_log: selection CACHE_HIT -> llm_selection="ok", llm_n_selected (no
       n_dropped: not recorded). Seed CACHE_HIT -> by the entry's provenance:
       llm -> "ok", fallback -> "fallback", missing/unknown -> NEW bucket
       "cached". llm_seeds_provenance recorded; llm_cached=True on any hit.
     - src/llm.py: seed CACHE_HIT line now carries provenance=<llm|fallback|
       unknown>; live LLM_SEEDGEN_OK writes seeds_provenance="llm" to the cache.
     - gen_llm_cache.py: tags seeds_provenance from the source log on build;
       new --backfill-provenance (dry run unless --write; never overwrites an
       existing tag; atomic) for the EXISTING cache.
     - summarize(): "LLM replay cache: N/M run(s) made no LLM call" and a !! line
       counting seeds of unknown provenance.
   VERIFIED on REAL VM logs (not fixtures): 3 leakcheck-fixed cache-hit logs
   (old format) -> ok/cached=True/n_selected 5,3,3/seeds=cached; live gpt41-corpus
   get_jobs -> unchanged ok/ok; live manage_docs -> ok/fallback. New-format e2e
   (arm prov-check, TEMP copy of 3 entries in ~/leakcheck/cache, real cache
   untouched): provenance=llm -> ok, =fallback -> fallback, =unknown -> cached.
   `--arm leakcheck-fixed --reparse`: selection unknown=3 -> ok=3.
   Old parse_log on the same real log -> unknown/None/unknown (test fails pre-fix).
   tests/test_parse_log_cache.py + tests/data/parse_log_real/*.txt (verbatim VM
   logs, .txt because .gitignore has *.log). Laptop suite 169 passed, 1 skipped.
   NOT DONE -- Ananto's call:
     (a) run `python3 eval/gen_llm_cache.py --arm gpt41-corpus --backfill-provenance
         --write` on the VM (tags 3670 llm + 12 fallback; 440 stay untagged ->
         "cached"). Metadata only, llm.py ignores the key, but it rewrites the
         live cache a running campaign reads.
     (b) should a cache entry holding FALLBACK seeds be replayed at all, or
         dropped so the LLM regenerates? Replaying keeps runs reproducible but
         fuzzes 3 deep24 configs from deterministic seeds.
     (c) --reparse existing arm indexes (it backs each up first).

